<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet>
    <title>Finding rules to describe numeric properties</title>

    <example xml:id="exA-LP">
        <title>The Locker Problem</title>

        <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    There are 20 lockers in a hallway numbered 1 through 20.
                    The lockers all start out closed.
                    Twenty students numbered one through 20 are given the
                    following instructions: Student 1 is instructed to go
                    through and open every locker.
                    Student 2 is instructed to start with locker 2 and close
                    every second locker.
                    Student 3 is instructed to start with locker 3 and change
                    the status of every third locker, that is, open a closed
                    locker and close an open locker.
                    Student 4 is instructed to start with locker 4 and change
                    the status of every fourth locker.
                    Students 5 through 20 are given similar instructions; what
                    lockers remain open after the 20th student has completed?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Suppose instead there were 100 lockers and 100 students;
                    what lockers remain open?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    For the scenario with 100 lockers and 100 students, which
                    locker(s) changed the most?
                    Which locker(s) changed the least?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find a prime factorization for the number 84.
                    How does the prime factorization relate to the locker
                    problem?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="exA-SF">
        <title>Sigma Function</title>

        <introduction>
            <p>
                In mathematics we can use function notation to describe rules.
                For example, the divisor function counts the number of divisors
                of a given number.
                We will use the Greek letter sigma <m>(\sigma)</m> to denote
                this function.
                So, for example, we can write <m>\sigma(6)=4,</m> because the
                number of divisors of 6 is 4, that is 6 is divisible by the
                numbers 1, 2, 3, and 6.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="0.25in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Evaluate <m>\sigma(12)=</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0.25in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Evaluate <m>\sigma(17)=</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <p>
                The prime factorization of a number can be used to help us
                determine the value of our sigma function.
                Consider the prime factorization of the number 72 which is
                <m>2^3\cdot3^2.</m> If we wanted to list all of the factors of
                72, we recognize that for any factor it can have either 0, 1, 2,
                or 3 factors of 2 and 0, 1, or 2 factors of 3.
                Therefore we see the factors of 72 are:
            </p>

            

                <tabular>
                    <row>
                        <cell><m>2^0\cdot3^0=1</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>2^0\cdot3^1=3</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>2^0\cdot3^2=9</m></cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell><m>2^1\cdot3^0=2</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>2^1\cdot3^1=6</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>2^1\cdot3^2=18</m></cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell><m>2^2\cdot3^0=4</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>2^2\cdot3^1=12</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>2^2\cdot3^2=36</m></cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell><m>2^3\cdot3^0=8</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>2^3\cdot3^1=24</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>2^3\cdot3^2=72</m></cell>
                    </row>
                </tabular>
            

            <p>
                Listing out in this manner we see that given 4 different options
                for the factor 2 and 3 different options for the factor 3, there
                are <m>4\cdot3=12</m> different factors for 72, and thus
                <m>\sigma(72)=12.</m>
            </p>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0.25in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Given that the prime factorization of 432 is
                    <m>2^4\cdot3^3</m> determine the value of
                    <m>\sigma(432).</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0.25in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find a number, <m>n</m> such that <m>\sigma(n)=6.</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="exA-CN">
        <title>Sum of consecutive numbers</title>

        <introduction>
            <p>
                In this example, we investigate which numbers can be written as
                the sum of consecutive positive integers.
                We can write <m>41</m> as the sum of two positive consecutive
                integers, <m>20+21.</m> We can write <m>54</m> as the sum of
                three positive consecutive integers, <m>17+18+19</m>.
                We can write <m>46</m> as the sum of 4 consecutive integers,
                <m>10+11+12+13</m>.
                What we are interested in investigating is whether every
                positive integer can be written as the sum of consecutive
                positive integers.
            </p>
        </introduction>

